### Construct with a computed name

`[?element]` builds an element whose head is computed; `[?attr]` and
`[?entry]` do the same for an attribute and a map entry.

{{EXAMPLE:program-element-dynamic-head-001-string block}}

### Build element content with `[?splice]`, never by dropping a sequence in

This is the single rule most likely to break the first program you write,
because every templating language you have seen splices a list into a slot
silently. **CX does not.** A non-empty sequence *value* in element-content
position is refused, and the diagnostic names the idiom you meant:

{{EXAMPLE:program-pvmatrix-026-construct-seq-refused block}}

{{EXAMPLE:program-pvmatrix-027-construct-splice-adopts block}}

Four consequences worth memorising:

* **`[?for]` and `[?splice]` contribute siblings; everything else contributes
  one child.** A binding read, a call result, an `[?if]` / `[?let]` /
  `[?match]` result each contribute exactly one — so one of those yielding a
  sequence refuses. Conditional multi-contribution is `[?splice [?if …]]`.
* **An argument is not content.** A `$`-head call, an operator head, or a
  closure call *consumes* a sequence argument happily. Only plain element
  construction refuses.
* **Absence contributes nothing.** `[c $u/nothing]` builds `[c]`, not an
  element with an empty child.
* **`[break]` / `[continue]` are exempt.** They are `[?loop]`'s positional
  value carriers, not documents: absence rides as the empty sequence and a
  sequence rides whole, because the loop rebinds positionally.

`reference-code-language.md` has the worked example for each.

### Transform with `[?modify]`

`[?modify]` rewrites a document by matching positions and replacing them. It
is the transform half of the language, and the reason CX needs no separate
template dialect.

{{EXAMPLE:program-modify-outer-pred-003-bound-outer-filters block}}

### Stream with the iterator family

`[?map]`, `[?filter]`, `[?take]`, `[?drop]`, `[?zip]`, `[?enumerate]`,
`[?chunks]` are lazy; a consumer forces them. `[?reduce]` folds.

{{EXAMPLE:program-iterator-lazy-002-take-bounds-output block}}

{{EXAMPLE:program-reduce-002-sequential-left-fold block}}

### Bring names in from data with `[?with-scope]`

{{EXAMPLE:program-with-scope-001-basic-merge block}}

### Sequence effects with `[?do]`

