## 10. Before you emit CX, check these

1. Every call has a `$`. A bare head is data — that is legal, and silent.
2. Flags come **before** the file; program arguments after it.
3. `[?let]` is flat. One `[?let]`, many `[= …]`, one body.
4. `[?if]` has `[then]` and `[else]` clause names.
5. Every directive you used is in the §4 registry.
6. Every module you imported is in the §8 catalog. You did not invent one.
7. Every effect has its `--allow-…` grant, and you granted the narrowest one.
8. Strings that must stay text are quoted.
9. No sequence value sits in element content. Where you meant many children,
   you wrote `[?splice …]` or `[?for …]`.
10. Every externalizing effect either carries no `[err]` or says
    `errs=:permit` — deliberately.
11. Where you compared for identity you used `cx hash` / canonical bytes, not
    `[$eq]`.
12. You did not write `[?try]`, `[?chain]`, `[?find]`, or `[?par-map]` — all
    retired.
13. You ran it. `cx FILE.cx` is one second; a plausible-looking CX program
    that does not parse costs the reader much more than that.

